                "role": "assistant",
                "content": "Thought: This problem requires us to place pawns on an $n \\times n$ board with specific constraints. We need to ensure no row or column has $k$ consecutive empty squares, and we're given the optimal number of pawns to achieve this. Since it's a constraint satisfaction problem with a combinatorial aspect, `run_ortools` seems like the best tool for this task.\nAction: run_ortools\n```python\nfrom ortools.sat.python import cp_model\n\ndef place_pawns(n, k):\n    model = cp_model.CpModel()\n\n    # Variables: x[i][j] is 1 if there's a pawn at position (i, j)\n    x = [[model.NewBoolVar(f'x_{i}_{j}') for j in range(n)] for i in range(n)]\n\n    # Constraint: No row or column should have k consecutive unoccupied squares\n    for i in range(n):\n        for j in range(n - k + 1):\n            # Row constraint\n            model.Add(sum(x[i][j + l] for l in range(k)) >= 1)\n\n            # Column constraint\n            model.Add(sum(x[j + l][i] for l in range(k)) >= 1)\n\n    # Objective: Minimize the number of pawns (given as 20)\n    objective = sum(x[i][j] for i in range(n) for j in range(n))\n    model.Add(objective == 20)\n\n    solver = cp_model.CpSolver()\n    status = solver.Solve(model)\n    \n    if status in (cp_model.OPTIMAL, cp_model.FEASIBLE):\n        return [[int(solver.Value(x[i][j])) for j in range(n)] for i in range(n)]\n    else:\n        return None\n\n# Given values\nn = 10\nk = 5\n\nresult = place_pawns(n, k)\n\nif result is not None:\n    print(result)\nelse:\n    print(\"No solution found.\")\n```\n"
